 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we derive that we act in commemoration of the Temple?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Source found in Jeremiah 30:17.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sukkah.41a.8",
   "daf": "41a",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מְנָא לַן דְּעָבְדִינַן זֵכֶר לַמִּקְדָּשׁ? אָמַר רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן, דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״כִּי אַעֲלֶה אֲרוּכָה לָךְ וּמִמַּכּוֹתַיִךְ אֶרְפָּאֵךְ נְאֻם ה׳ כִּי נִדָּחָה קָרְאוּ לָךְ צִיּוֹן הִיא דּוֹרֵשׁ אֵין לָהּ״. ״דּוֹרֵשׁ אֵין לָהּ״, מִכְּלַל דְּבָעֲיָא דְּרִישָׁה.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive that «we institute» ordinances in «commemoration of the Temple? Rabbi Yoḥanan said» that it is «as the verse states: “For I will restore health unto you and I will heal you of your wounds, says the Lord; because they have called you an outcast, she is Zion, there is none that seeks her”» (Jeremiah 30:17). From the fact that the verse states: “There is none that seeks her,” it can be learned «by inference that it requires seeking,» i.e., people should think of and remember the Temple. That is the reason for Rabban Yoḥanan ben Zakkai’s ordinance."
  }
 ],
